      "source": [
        "\n",
        "Title:\n",
        "# **Agrivoltaics Microclimate Analysis and Visualization in Python**\n",
        "\n",
        "Text:\n",
        "This is a full statistical and graphical data analysis for my PhD experimental study in Agrivoltaics microclimates in West Africa comparing two bioclimates i.e. Sudan (Tamale) and Guidean (Kumasi).\n",
        "the datasets are stored in Excel.xlsx formate. Tne datasets contains multi-sensor environmental data recorded under different treatments, crops, and locations. The data legend is as follows:\n",
        "\n",
        "Variables:\n",
        "* SM: Volumetric soil moisture content (%)\n",
        "* ST: Soil temperature (°C)\n",
        "* AH: Relative humidity of air within canopy (%)\n",
        "* AT: Air temperature within canopy (°C)\n",
        "* PI: PV solar radiation (W/m²)\n",
        "* PT: PV panel temperature (°C)\n",
        "* RH: Ambient relative humidity (%)\n",
        "* T: Ambient air temperature (°C)\n",
        "* P: Precipitation (mm)\n",
        "Treatments:\n",
        "* AV: Agrivoltaic plots\n",
        "* AG: Agriculture-only plots\n",
        "* PV: Photovoltaic-only plots\n",
        "* WS: Weather Station (reference)\n",
        "Crops:\n",
        "* T: Tomato\n",
        "* P: Pepper\n",
        "* E: Eggplant\n",
        "Locations:\n",
        "* Tamale (Sudan/savannah zone)\n",
        "* Kumasi (Guinean/forest zone)\n",
        "\n",
        "Goals:\n",
        "To perform an analysis including data cleaning, descriptive and inferential statistics, multivariate modeling, and advanced visualizations.\n",
        "To achieve this goal, we performed the following sequentially:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Data Preparation\n",
        "* Load and preview the Excel data (all sheets).\n",
        "* Clean missing values, remove duplicates, and perform consistency checks.\n",
        "* Apply correct units and data types.\n",
        "* Compute derived metrics (e.g., VPD, daily averages, or temperature anomalies).\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Descriptive Statistics\n",
        "* Compute summary statistics (mean, SD, range, CV) per variable, treatment, and location.\n",
        "* Generate correlation matrices and pairwise scatterplots to explore relationships.\n",
        "* Create clean tables of descriptive results (exportable to CSV or Excel).\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Comparative Analysis\n",
        "* Conduct two-way and three-way ANOVA to test effects of Treatment, Location, and Crop on each variable (SM, ST, AT, AH, etc.).\n",
        "* Add Tukey HSD post-hoc tests for pairwise comparisons.\n",
        "* Visualize results using boxplots, violin plots, and grouped bar charts with significance annotations.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. Time-Series and Temporal Analysis\n",
        "* Generate daily and hourly time-series plots for key parameters.\n",
        "* Apply rolling averages or seasonal decomposition (statsmodels).\n",
        "* Conduct trend analysis using MannKendall tests and Sens slope estimators.\n",
        "* Visualize diurnal patterns using line charts or heatmaps.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. Multivariate and Advanced Modeling\n",
        "* Perform Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to detect major environmental gradients.\n",
        "* Run Cluster Analysis (HCA or k-means) to classify treatment responses.\n",
        "* Fit Multiple Linear Regression or Lasso Regression models linking PV variables (PI, PT) to microclimate variables (AT, AH, ST, SM).\n",
        "* Optionally include Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) using the semopy or pingouin package.\n",
        "\n",
        "6. Visualization and Graphical Output\n",
        "Create professional, publication-ready figures:\n",
        "* Boxplots and violin plots by treatment and location.\n",
        "* 3D surface plots (matplotlib or plotly) showing interactions (e.g., PIATSM).\n",
        "* Heatmaps for correlations and diurnal changes.\n",
        "* Energy balance schematics or microclimate profiles under PV structures.\n",
        "* Geospatial interpolation maps (if coordinates available).\n",
        "Ensure all figures:\n",
        "* Have clear axis labels, legends, and titles.\n",
        "* Use consistent color schemes (e.g., blue for AV, green for AG, orange for PV, gray for WS).\n",
        "* Have export options (.png, .pdf, .svg) in publication quality.\n",
        "\n",
        "7. Output and Documentation\n",
        "* Export all summary tables and model outputs to Excel/CSV.\n",
        "* Save all figures in a Results_Figures folder.\n",
        "* Provide a short Markdown summary for each section describing the interpretation of outputs.\n",
        "\n",
        "8. Optional Extensions\n",
        "* Use plotly or dash to create an interactive dashboard to visualize AgriPV microclimate dynamics.\n",
        "* Integrate seaborn or matplotlib styles for consistent publication quality (Nature or Elsevier theme).\n",
        "\n",
        "Important Notes:\n",
        "* Use pandas, numpy, matplotlib, seaborn, scipy, statsmodels, pingouin, sklearn, and semopy packages.\n",
        "* Write code that is modular, clean, and reusable.\n",
        "* Include explanatory comments (Markdown cells) summarizing each analysis purpose and how to interpret results.\n",
        "* Focus on replicability and statistical soundness.\n",
        "\n",
