      "source": [
        "## Summary:\n",
        "\n",
        "## Summary of Data Analysis for Agrivoltaics Microclimate Study\n",
        "\n",
        "### Data Analysis Key Findings\n",
        "\n",
        "*   **Data Loading and Preparation:** Data from \"Tamale\\_cleaned.xlsx\" and \"Kumasi\\_cleaned.xlsx\" was successfully loaded, with 22464 entries and 37 columns each. Data cleaning involved combining 'Date' and 'Time' into a 'Datetime' index, handling missing values using forward fill, and standardizing column names.\n",
        "*   **Descriptive Statistics and Location Comparison:**\n",
        "    *   Kumasi showed higher mean volumetric soil water moisture content and soil temperatures across all three crops compared to Tamale.\n",
        "    *   Tamale had higher mean PV solar radiation, air temperature, and slightly higher mean precipitation.\n",
        "    *   Kumasi had significantly higher mean relative humidity than Tamale.\n",
        "*   **Diurnal and Seasonal Patterns:**\n",
        "    *   Both locations exhibited typical diurnal patterns with temperature and solar radiation peaking during the day and relative humidity peaking at night.\n",
        "    *   Seasonal patterns showed variations in temperature, solar radiation, relative humidity, and soil moisture across the months.\n",
        "*   **Correlation Analysis:**\n",
        "    *   A strong negative correlation was observed between air temperature and relative humidity in both locations.\n",
        "    *   Solar radiation was positively correlated with air and soil temperatures.\n",
        "    *   Soil temperatures were positively correlated with air temperature.\n",
        "*   **Vapor Pressure Deficit (VPD):**\n",
        "    *   VPD generally peaked during midday to afternoon in both locations, indicating the period of highest potential plant water stress.\n",
        "    *   Tamale consistently showed higher VPD values throughout the day compared to Kumasi, suggesting greater atmospheric water demand in Tamale.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Insights or Next Steps\n",
        "\n",
        "*   The observed differences in key environmental parameters between Tamale and Kumasi (e.g., temperature, humidity, solar radiation, soil moisture) highlight the significant microclimate variations that can influence crop growth and Agrivoltaics system performance across different regions.\n",
